<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_text-file-applications-and-file-modes">
    <title>Text-File Applications and File Modes</title>

    <p>
        A complete text-file application separates file mechanics from the work
        performed on each record. The program opens the input, reads one line at
        a time, parses and validates the line, performs an operation, and writes
        a report when needed.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table_chapter12_file-modes">
        <title>Useful text-file modes</title>
        <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
            <col width="25%"/><col width="75%"/>
            <row header="yes">
                <cell>Mode</cell>
                <cell>Purpose</cell>
            </row>
            <row><cell>Default output</cell><cell>Create or replace the existing contents.</cell></row>
            <row><cell><c>std::ios::app</c></cell><cell>Write at the end without replacing existing contents.</cell></row>
            <row><cell><c>std::ios::in | std::ios::out</c></cell><cell>Open a stream for both reading and writing when supported by the stream type.</cell></row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_score-analyzer">
        <title>Complete CSV score analyzer</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_score-analyzer"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

struct ScoreRecord {
    std::string name;
    int score{};
};

bool parse_score_record(const std::string& line, ScoreRecord& record) {
    std::istringstream record_stream{line};
    std::string name;
    int score{};

    if (!std::getline(record_stream, name, ',')) {
        return false;
    }
    if (!(record_stream >> score)) {
        return false;
    }
    record_stream >> std::ws;

    if (!record_stream.eof() || name.empty() || score < 0 || score > 100) {
        return false;
    }

    record = ScoreRecord{name, score};
    return true;
}

int main() {
    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.csv"};
    std::ofstream report_file{"score-report.txt"};

    if (!input_file || !report_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    std::string line;
    ScoreRecord record;
    int count{};
    int total{};

    while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {
        if (parse_score_record(line, record)) {
            ++count;
            total += record.score;
        }
        else {
            std::cerr << "Skipping malformed record: " << line << '\n';
        }
    }

    report_file << "Valid records: " << count << '\n';
    if (count > 0) {
        report_file << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2)
                    << "Average score: "
                    << static_cast<double>(total) / count << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_inventory-input">
        <title>Example inventory file</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_inventory-input"
                 language="text" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
1001,Notebook,12,4.50
1002,Pen,40,1.25
1003,Backpack,5,32.00
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
        An inventory application could parse each line into an item record and
        calculate <c>quantity * unit_price</c>. The same design applies: keep
        parsing, validation, calculation, and reporting as separate tasks.
    </p>

    <exercise label="chapter12_4_append">
        <statement><p>When should a program use <c>std::ios::app</c>?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p>When it must replace all existing contents</p></statement><feedback><p>Default output commonly replaces existing contents.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>When new output must be added at the end of an existing file</p></statement><feedback><p>Append mode preserves earlier contents.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>When it wants to read only from a keyboard</p></statement><feedback><p><c>app</c> is a file mode.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>When it wants to force binary representation</p></statement><feedback><p>Binary mode is selected separately with <c>std::ios::binary</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>When the file must be deleted automatically</p></statement><feedback><p>Append mode does not delete files.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_4_application-design">
        <statement><p>Select all useful separations of responsibility in a text-file application.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Use one function to parse one record.</p></statement><feedback><p>A small parser is easier to test.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Let the file loop decide how to handle malformed records.</p></statement><feedback><p>The caller can report, skip, or stop as appropriate.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Put every task into one very long loop body.</p></statement><feedback><p>Large mixed responsibilities are harder to test and reuse.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Assume every line is valid because the file extension is CSV.</p></statement><feedback><p>A filename extension does not validate contents.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Use <c>std::cin</c> inside the parser to repair bad file data.</p></statement><feedback><p>Parsing a file should receive its input rather than unexpectedly ask the user.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_4_application-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to open an input file, open a report, and process each line.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>std::ifstream input_file{"scores.csv"};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>std::ofstream report_file{"report.txt"};</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>if (!input_file || !report_file) { return 1; }</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>std::string line;</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>    report_file &lt;&lt; line &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="7"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
